hex2: emit a label map, if asked for.

hex2 produces a bare ELF -- no sections, no symbol table. That is right for a
bootstrap linker and it makes a crash unreadable: a fault reports an address
and there is nothing to turn it into a name.

Every diagnosis in spikes/stage3 has worked around that by bisecting with
write() markers, because a raw program counter was useless. That found real
bugs, but it can only bracket BETWEEN probe points, and on the last hunt it
narrowed to a few statements and stopped. Four instrument bugs during that hunt
produced false negatives that were acted on -- a stale slot address, a static
function called cross-file, a watch that reset its baseline on the first hit,
and a print taken before the field was populated. Sampling is not only slow
here, it fails quietly.

THE ADDRESS IS ALREADY COMPUTED. storeLabel is handed `ip` and records it; this
writes the same number out as it is assigned. The alternative -- rebuilding the
table by counting bytes over the .hex2 -- means reimplementing the width of
every directive for every architecture, and being wrong the first time the two
implementations drift. Taking the number from the assembler that computes it
cannot drift.

OFF BY DEFAULT. Nothing happens unless HEX2_LABEL_MAP names a file. Checked by
building both ways: the emitted binary is byte-identical with the variable set
and unset, so this cannot change what is being debugged.

    14626 labels for the current mc-tcc
    804DC0 FUNCTION_tok_alloc_new
    85CEA0 FUNCTION_post_type

WHAT IT IS FOR. A native gdb on the aarch64 runner can set a hardware
watchpoint on the address a stray write is landing on -- but without a symbol
table its backtrace is bare hex. This is the other half of that.

It is NOT for the emulator. qemu-user's gdbstub implements breakpoints and
refuses watchpoints:

    Z0 (sw breakpoint) -> OK
    Z1 (hw breakpoint) -> OK
    Z2 (write watch)   -> ''

so watchpoint work has to happen on real aarch64 hardware, which is what the
runner already is.

diff --git a/hex2.c b/hex2.c
index 0e3c85a..8bd6833 100644
--- a/hex2.c
+++ b/hex2.c
@@ -18,6 +18,8 @@
  */
 
 #include "hex2_globals.h"
+#include <stdlib.h>
+extern FILE* label_map_file;
 
 /* The essential functions */
 void first_pass(struct input_files* input);
@@ -36,6 +38,8 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
 
 	Architecture = KNIGHT;
 	Base_Address = 0;
+	{ char* _lm = getenv("HEX2_LABEL_MAP");
+	  if(NULL != _lm) label_map_file = fopen(_lm, "w"); }
 	struct input_files* input = NULL;
 	output = stdout;
 	char* output_file = "";
diff --git a/hex2_linker.c b/hex2_linker.c
index 7785692..02ecd76 100644
--- a/hex2_linker.c
+++ b/hex2_linker.c
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@ FILE* output;
 struct entry** jump_tables;
 int BigEndian;
 int Base_Address;
+FILE* label_map_file;
 int Architecture;
 int ByteMode;
 int exec_enable;
@@ -146,6 +147,31 @@ int storeLabel(FILE* source_file, int ip)
 	entry->next = jump_tables[h];
 	jump_tables[h] = entry;
 
+	/* A LABEL MAP, IF ASKED FOR.
+	 *
+	 * hex2 emits a bare ELF: no sections, no symbol table. That is correct
+	 * for a bootstrap linker and it makes a crash unreadable -- a fault
+	 * reports an address and there is nothing to turn it into a name. Every
+	 * debugging session in spikes/stage3 has worked around it by hand,
+	 * bisecting with write() markers because a raw program counter was
+	 * useless.
+	 *
+	 * This is the address hex2 has ALREADY computed for the label, written
+	 * out as it is assigned. Building the same table independently -- by
+	 * counting bytes over the .hex2 -- would mean reimplementing every
+	 * directive's width and would be wrong the first time the two drifted.
+	 * Taking it from the assembler that computes it cannot drift.
+	 *
+	 * Off unless HEX2_LABEL_MAP names a file, so the default output and the
+	 * default behaviour are unchanged. */
+	if(NULL != label_map_file)
+	{
+		fputs(int2str(entry->target + Base_Address, 16, FALSE), label_map_file);
+		fputc(' ', label_map_file);
+		fputs(entry->name, label_map_file);
+		fputc('\n', label_map_file);
+	}
+
 	return c;
 }
 
